    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Brings Fresh Adventure-Ready Concepts at the 2026 SEMA Show Under the Woodland Banner\nDealer Hopes To Get Over $50K for This Pontiac Trans Am, Price Actually Reduced\n45 photos\nPhoto: Showdown Motors on eBay\nTrans Am is the name, and 1979 is the model year. A combination that you really can’t ignore, especially considering what the 1979 model year represented for the evolution of the Pontiac Trans Am.\nHowever, this specimen is no longer the all-original specimen that so many people would park in their garage. It’s a car whose only mission is to find a buyer and then be tucked away under a cover to only see the daylight on sunny Sundays.\nThe only problem could be the price. The dealer claims this is actually a discount, so if your negotiation skills are top-notch, maybe you can get an even better deal.\nPhoto: Showdown Motors on eBay Meanwhile, I can’t help but remind everybody out there that the 1979 model year was the year for the Trans Am. Pontiac celebrated the nameplate’s 10th anniversary, and the way it decided to do this made perfect sense.\nThe Tenth Anniversary Trans Am was, well, an anniversary model. It was the first configuration to cost more than $10K, but this made perfect sense. Every TATA came with every option in the entire lineup, so at the end of the day, you were getting fantastic value for the money.\nBuyers could only choose the engine, with two options offered: the Pontiac 400 with a manual transmission and the Oldsmobile 403 with an automatic. This means that you can figure out what engine is under the hood by simply checking the transmission.\nPhoto: Showdown Motors on eBay While the production of the TATA was limited to 7,500 units, Pontiac ended the year with over 117K Trans Ams rolling off the assembly lines. It was the best year in the history of the Trans Am, and the timing was perfect, considering this was when it celebrated its 10th anniversary.\nThe base Firebird , which continues to be the most affordable version in the lineup, was the runner-up with 38,642 cars, followed by the Esprit with 30,853 units. The Formula was the least desirable, despite coming with a 301 two-barrel standard and the Trans Am engines optionally, with only 24,850 orders.\nThis Trans Am doesn’t sport the desirable TATA treatment, but the black package makes it a head-turning machine anyway. Before you ask, no, the paint is not original, as someone resprayed it before the car landed online.\nThe dealer doesn’t offer more specifics, but the Trans Am also sports a custom-painted bird on the hood and 17-inch wheels.\nThe cabin is also in almost perfect shape, and this makes perfect sense, considering everything in there is completely new. I don’t think you’ll find anything to complain about, and my recommendation is that you call the dealer and arrange an in-person inspection before you make an offer.\nOn the other hand, I believe that it’s a shame that this Trans Am has stepped away so far from the original configuration. While I’m a big fan of original vehicles, I’m not entirely against aftermarket upgrades, unless they don’t make much sense.\nPhoto: Showdown Motors on eBay This could be the case of the Pioneer radio in this Trans Am, though the seller reveals that it’s part of an upgraded audio system that also includes Pioneer speakers.\nThe car comes with a Hurst dual-gate shifter, power steering, and front power disc brakes.\nAs for the engine under the hood, the Trans Am is fitted with a 6.6-liter V8 paired with an automatic 3-speed transmission. It’s safe to assume that everything is in perfect shape, so if you want to hop in and drive, you should be able to do this during your live inspection.\nFurthermore, I don’t think this Trans Am would require further investments, so if you’re the adventurous type, you can fly in and drive home in your new Pontiac. Just make sure it won’t be raining or anything, as it’d be a shame to damage this Trans Am after all these years.\nPhoto: Showdown Motors on eBay As for the price, this is where things are getting tricky. The vehicle doesn’t require further investments, but on the other hand, it also looks to be too expensive for someone who is in the market for a non-original car. Sure, it checks many boxes for an occasional driver, and that’s why I recommended that you go there in person, but many people might walk away when hearing how much this Trans Am costs.\nThe dealer wants $52,900 for this Trans Am, but they also enabled the Make Offer option on eBay, so call them and discuss the rest of the details if you’re interested in the car. A walkaround video would also come in handy, considering many other details are missing, especially regarding the underside.\nThe vehicle is parked in Fraser, Michigan, and considering the listing expires in 6 days, you should first convince your significant other that you need another Trans Am and then pack your bags for a trip to the dealer’s location to see the car with your own eyes.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTrans Am Pontiac pontiac trans am 1979 Trans Am\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT Here’s How Much a 2024 Lamborghini Urus Performante Costs After 9,000 Miles",
